    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int {(\\frac{1}{15})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(\\frac{1}{15})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{15})}^{x}}{\\ln (\\frac{1}{15})}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the laws of logarithms, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln (\\frac{1}{15}) & =\\ln 1-\\ln 15 \\\\  & =0-\\ln 15 \\\\  & =-\\ln 15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify our final answer as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{15})}^{x}}{\\ln (\\frac{1}{15})}+C=-\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{15})}^{x}}{\\ln 15}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/id": "7b6c4403-3ace-5274-ab47-4981f67c6270",
